# The public half of the key that signs update manifests, pinned here so a node verifies
# a release against something it was provisioned with rather than against a key supplied
# by the same server that supplied the release. That is the whole point: a signature
# checked against a key from the same response proves nothing.
#
# Generate the pair on the release workstation and paste the public half below. The
# private half belongs on that workstation only, and on no cluster node, ever:
#
#   openssl genpkey -algorithm ed25519 -out ~/.helios/release_ed25519.key
#   chmod 600 ~/.helios/release_ed25519.key
#   openssl pkey -in ~/.helios/release_ed25519.key -pubout -out ~/.helios/release_ed25519.pub
#
# Left empty deliberately. An empty key fails closed -- check-updates reports an error
# and offers no update -- which is the safe direction, and nothing else stops working.
# Shipping a key whose private half nobody holds would be worse: it looks configured and
# can never verify anything.
